## ONLINE METHODS
Cell maintenance and reprogramming. MEFs (inducible 6C, 1B lines)11 and MEFs stably expressing the reverse tetracycline transactivator (rtTA) (Rosa26 rtTA-IRES-GFP knock-in)26 were maintained in Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (DMEM) supplemented with \(10\%\) (v/v) FBS (Gibco), \(1~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium pyruvate (Gibco), \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) Glutamax (Invitrogen) and \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin (Invitrogen). Mouse fibroblasts were reprogrammed either in serum containing mouse ESC medium consisting of DMEM supplemented with \(15\%\) (v/v) FBS (Wisent), \(0.1\ \mathrm{mM}\) \(\upbeta\) -mercaptoethanol (BME; Sigma), \(1~\mathrm{\mM}\) sodium pyruvate, \(0.1\ \mathrm{mM}\) MEM non-essential amino acid (NEAA, Gibco), \(2~\mathrm{mM}\) Glutamax, \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin-streptomycin and \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{U}\ \mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) of leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF) (Millipore) or in serum-free ESC (SFES) medium consisting of DMEM with F12 (Gibco) and Neurobasal (Gibco)-based medium supplemented with N2 (Gibco), B27 (Gibco), \(0.05\%\) (w/v) BSA, \(2~\mathrm{mM}\) Glutamax, \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin, \(1.5\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{M}\) monothioglycerol, \(1000\;\mathrm{U}\;\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) LIF, and \(10\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) BMP4 (ref. 18). Mouse ESC lines (R1 and rtTA-GFP) were maintained in mouse ESC medium.
To reprogram in adherent conditions, secondary inducible 6C MEFs were plated in gelatin-coated six-well plates (Sarstedt) and induced with \(1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) doxycycline (Sigma) the day after. Medium exchanges were carried out every \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) during the entire reprogramming process. Primary mouse fibroblasts were transduced with respective viral preparations \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and \(36\,\mathrm{h}\) after seeding. Culture medium was supplemented with doxycycline \(24~\mathrm{h}\) after the last viral transduction, and cells were reprogrammed following the same protocols as for secondary fibroblasts.
To reprogram in suspension conditions, secondary inducible 6C MEFs were trypsinized \(8\,\mathrm{h}\) after doxycycline induction and seeded either into Sigmacote (Sigma)-treated spinner flasks (Integra Biosciences) at \(0.5\times10^{5}\) to \(1\times10^{5}\) cells \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) or in low-cell-binding plates (Nunc). Primary mouse fibroblasts were transduced with viral preparations \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and \(36\,\mathrm{h}\) after seeding. Cultures were supplemented with doxycycline \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) after the last viral transduction. Eight to twelve hours after induction (doxycycline addition), cells were trypsinized and seeded into spinner flasks at \(2\times10^{5}\,\mathrm{cells\,ml^{-1}}\) . Culture volumes were between \(30{-}50\;\mathrm{ml}\) , cultured with a constant stirring speed of \(65\;\mathrm{r.p.m}\) . One-third of the culture medium was replaced every day. Spinner flasks were replaced every \(6\,\mathrm{d}\) to prevent sticking of cells to vessel walls. To remove large aggregates from high-density cultures, cells were passed through \(100{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}\) cell strainers (BD Biosciences). All adherent cultures, spinner flasks and low-cell-binding plates were incubated in a humidified \(5\%\) (v/v) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) air environment at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
Suspension reprogramming of secondary chimera-derived spleen cells and T-cell progenitors. Spleen of secondary chimeric adult 1B mice was isolated, minced and cultured in minimal essential medium alpha (αMEM) (Gibco) containing \(16.7\%\) (v/v) FBS (HyClone), \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin, \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\) \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) human IL-2 and \(5\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) mouse granulocyte macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) (R&D Systems) to promote survival. The cells were seeded at \(1\times10^{6}\) cells \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) density in low-adhesion plates (Nunc) in the presence of doxycycline for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\) . After \(8\,\mathrm{h}\) , cells were seeded at \(1\times10^{5}\) cells \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) in spinner flasks in αMEM with cytokines and doxycycline as above or at a density of \(5\times10^{4}\) cells per six-well plate on irradiated feeder MEFs. After \(2\,\mathrm{d}\) ,
the medium was replaced with fresh αMEM supplemented with \(40\%\) mouse ESC medium. Once the cells started forming aggregates (after \(4\mathrm{~d~}\) ), stirring was initiated in the spinner flasks at \(65~\mathrm{r.p.m}\) ., and the medium was replaced to \(100\%\) ESC medium containing IL-2, GM-CSF and doxycycline. Blood cytokines were completely removed after \(8\,\mathrm{d}\) . Doxycycline was removed after \(15\,\mathrm{d}\) for expansion of spleen-derived SiPSCs.
For isolation of double-negative (DN1) progenitor T cells, the thymus of secondary chimeric adult 1B mice was isolated, minced and stained for \(\mathrm{CD4^{-}C D8^{-}O D25^{-}C D44^{+}}\) surface-marker expression. FACS-separated DN1-T cells were cultured in lowadhesion plates (Nunc) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) in αMEM containing \(16.7\%\) (v/v) FBS (HyClone), \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin, \(50\,\mathrm{ng\,ml^{-1}}\) mouse stem cell factor (SCF), \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) human FLT3L and \(10\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) mouse IL-7 (R&D Systems) to promote survival and doxycycline \((1\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1})\) to initiate reprogramming. After \(2\,\mathrm{d}\) , the medium was replaced with fresh αMEM supplemented with \(40\%\) (v/v) mouse ESC medium. After \(4\mathrm{~d~}\) , the medium was exchanged with \(100\%\) mouse ESC medium containing SCF, FLT3L, IL-7 and doxycycline. After \(6\,\mathrm{~d~}\) , reprogramming DN1 T cells started forming aggregates and stirring was initiated. ESC medium was replaced with half the concentration of blood cytokines, which were completely removed after \(8\mathrm{~d~}\) . Doxycycline was removed after \(15\,\mathrm{~d~}\) for expansion of DN1 cell-derived SiPSCs.
Integrated production of cardiac progenitors. RtTA MEFs were transduced in T75 flasks at day 0 and day 1. Cells were induced with doxycycline at day 2, and \(^{8\,\mathrm{h}}\) after induction were shifted to suspension conditions in spinner flasks. After day 10, cells were diluted to concentrations below \(10^{6}\) cells \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) to avoid medium depletion. At day 16, cells were shifted from mouse ESC medium with doxycycline to SFES medium without doxycycline and incubated in spinner flasks or in low-adherence \(10{\mathrm{-cm}}\) Petri dishes (Fisher) placed on an orbital shaker \((65{-}75\;\mathrm{r.p.m.})\) . Day-19 cultures were passed through a \(40{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}\) strainer (BD Biosciences), and filter retentates were used for cardiac induction as described previously18. Input populations were either non-dissociated aggregates or single cells after trypsin dissociation. Differentiation to cTnT cells was performed by dissociating day-21 cultures and re-aggregating single cells as 100 cell aggregates in \(400{\mathrm{-}}{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) microwell inserts27 in serum-free differentiation (SFD) medium18 supplemented with \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) BMP4 (R&D), \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) Activin A (R&D) and \(5\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) VEGF (Sigma) for \(3\,\mathrm{d}\) . On day 24, aggregates were transferred from microwells to 6-cm plates containing Stempro medium (Invitrogen) supplemented with \(150~\mathrm{ng~ml^{-1}}\) DKK1 (R&D), \(5\;\mathrm{ng}\;\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) VEGF \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) bFGF (Peprotech) and \(10\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) FGF10, and cultured for an additional \(5\,\mathrm{d}\) and then analyzed by FACS.
Small molecules used were 5-azacytidine \(\mathrm{3\,\upmuM}\) , Sigma), ROCK inhibitor (Y27632, \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , Sigma), PD0325901 ( \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , Stemgent), CHIR99021 \(\mathrm{~3~}\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , Stemgent). The 2i medium consisted of SFES medium without BMP4, supplemented with PD0325901 and CHIR99021.
The amplified cMyc and Sox2 fragments were subcloned by ligating the NheI and NotI–digested inserts into the corresponding sites of pCEP4 (Invitrogen). Fragments were excised from these intermediates by NheI and XhoI restriction and subsequently ligated into the corresponding sites of pMF351 (ref. 28) generating PDFCMV-cMyc and PDFCMV-Sox2. In a second step the Tet promoter (PTET) was amplified by PCR from PB-TET-Myc11 using primers PTET_fwd and PTET_rev, restricted using AscI and NheI and subsequently ligated into the AscI and NheI–digested pDFCMV-Myc and pDFCMV-Sox2 backbones.
PDFTET-KOS was generated by PCR-amplifying KOS from PB-TET-MKOS11 using primers Klf4_fwd and Sox2_rev. The KOS-containing fragment was restricted using NheI and NotI and ligated into the corresponding sites of PDFTET-Sox2.
Viral particles were produced by transfection of HEK293 T cells with calcium phosphate–DNA precipitates. Medium was changed to Advanced DMEM (Gibco) supplemented with \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin, \(1\%\) (v/v) Glutamax, \(2\%\) (v/v) FBS (Gibco) and \(0.01\,\mathrm{\mM}\) cholesterol \(16~\mathrm{h}\) after transfection. Supernatants were collected twice, 36 and \(60~\mathrm{h}\) after transfection, filtered through a \(0.45\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}\) filter, aliquoted and stored at \(-80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . For standard transfections, \(8\,\mathrm{ml}\) of MEF culture medium was mixed with \(400~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of each viral supernatant and added to MEF cells grown in T75 tissue culture flasks in the presence of \(8\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) Polybrene (Sigma).
Microarray analysis. Whole-genome expression analysis of cells reprogramming under adherent and suspension conditions was carried out by isolating total RNA at indicated time points using RNAeasy RNA isolation kits (Qiagen). Hybridizations were carried out on Affymetrix mouse Gene 1.0 ST arrays (one array per time point and condition) and normalized using RMA (Affymetrix expression console). Fold changes and Pearson correlation coefficients were calculated in Excel (Microsoft). Hierarchical clustering was performed using Cluster 3.0 (similarity metric was correlation (centered); clustering method was average linkage) and visualized by JavaTree software. GO term enrichment analysis was performed using the database for annotation, visualization and integrated discovery (DAVID) bioinformatics resource29.
Differentiation protocols. Mesoderm and endoderm differentiation was carried out by dissociating iPSC and seeding in lowadhesion plates at a density of \(1\times10^{6}\) cells per \(10\,\mathrm{ml}\) in DMEM containing \(15\%\) (v/v) FBS, \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin, \(2\;\mathrm{mM}\) Glutamax, \(0.1\;\mathrm{mM}\) BME and \(0.1\;\mathrm{mM}\) NEAA. Cells were cultured for \(4\:\mathrm{d}\) on an orbital shaker (65 r.p.m.) with medium exchange at day 2. After 4 d, suspension aggregates were seeded
on gelatin-coated tissue-culture plates and cultured for another 5 d before staining with antibodies.
Ectoderm differentiation was performed according to ref. 30. Briefly, iPSCs were trypsinized and plated at \(5\,\times\,10^{5}\) cells per \(10~\mathrm{ml}\) in SFEB medium (Glasgow minimum essential medium (GMEM) supplemented with \(5\%\) (v/v) knockout serum replacement, \(0.1\,\mathrm{mM}\) NEAA, \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) sodium pyruvate, \(1\%\) (v/v) penicillin and streptomycin and \(0.1\,\mathrm{mM}\) BME). Cells were cultured for \(3\,\mathrm{d}\) in low-adherence plates. At day 3 cells were re-fed by replacing \(70\%\) of the medium and cultured for another \(2\,\mathrm{d}\) . Spheres were transferred intact to Matrigel-coated six-well plates and incubated for \(5\,\mathrm{d}\) in N2B27 medium (DMEM with F12 and Neurobasal medium supplemented with B27, N2 supplements, \(0.005\%\) (w/v) BSA and \(1\;\mathrm{mM}\) sodium pyruvate) before staining for TUBB3 expression.
Immunocytochemistry stainings were performed by fixing cells in PBS containing \(4\%\) (v/v) formaldehyde. Cells were permeabilized in PBS containing \(0.1\%\) (v/v) Triton X-100 and subsequently blocked in PBS containing \(10\%\) (v/v) donkey serum. Samples were incubated with primary and secondary antibodies in PBS containing \(1\%\) (w/v) BSA and imaged using a confocal microscope (FV1000 laser scanning confocal; Olympus) with \(5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) optical sections. Images represent the \(z\) -stack projection of five to ten confocal optical sections. Annexin V (Invitrogen) staining was carried out according to the manufacturer's protocol. EdU cell proliferation assays were performed according to the manufacturer's protocol (Invitrogen). Calcein-AM and ethidium homodimer I staining (Sigma) was performed as indicated in the manufacturer's protocol.
Generation of chimeras. Doxycycline-independent suspension iPSCs were plated and collected as cell clumps of 8–15 cells from gelatinized dishes by gentle trypsinization. For diploid chimeras, \(2.5~\mathrm{d}\) post-coitum (d.p.c.) Hsd:ICR(CD-1) embryos were aggregated with iPSC clumps and cultured overnight at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(5\%\) (v/v) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in potassium simplex optimized medium (KSOM) (Millipore). Embryos were transferred into pseudopregnant recipient ICR females \(24~\mathrm{h}\) later. For \(\beta\)-galactosidase detection, pregnant dams were treated with doxycycline \((1.5~\mathrm{mg~ml^{-1}}\) doxycycline; \(5\%\) (w/v) sucrose in water) \(20~\mathrm{h}\) before dissection. \(\beta\)-galactosidase staining and sectioning were performed as described in ref. 11. The mice were housed in a pathogen-free environment and the care of the animals was in accordance with institutional guidelines (Toronto Centre for Phenogenomics).
Statistical analysis. Results were expressed as mean \(\pm\) s.d. from at least three independent replicates if not otherwise stated. Data were checked for normalcy using the Kolmogorov-Smirnov
test with \(P=0.05\). Multiple comparisons were performed using ANOVA with Tukey post-hoc comparisons to determine significant differences between groups \((P<0.05\), two-tailed). Results were confirmed using non-parametric Mann-Whitney U tests. Calculations were performed using Minitab 16.2.1 (Minitab).
